  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-4,$ ${x}_{2}=1,$ and ${x}_{3}=2,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $3.$ Find $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-4,$ ${x}_{2}=1,$ and ${x}_{3}=2,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =3(x-(-4))(x-1)(x-2) \\\\  & =3(x+4)(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+4)(x-1)(x-2) & =3({x}^{2}+3x-4)(x-2) \\\\  & =3({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-6x-4x+8) \\\\  & =3({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-30x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-30x+24.$",
